# {{ name | kebab_case }}

An API-only Axum service generated by the `axum` Moon template. It does not
implement browser login or sessions. A TanStack or Next.js app generated from
this workspace sends an access token in the `Authorization: Bearer` header;
this API validates the token with ZITADEL introspection before it provides the
verified `sub` to a protected handler.

## Endpoints

- `GET /healthz` is public and returns `{"status":"ok"}`.
- `GET /api/me` requires an active ZITADEL access token with this project's
  audience and returns the verified subject, the user's display name when
  ZITADEL has one, and their roles: `{"sub":"...","name":"...","roles":["..."]}`.

{% if database %}- `GET /api/notes` and `POST /api/notes` list and add the signed-in user's own
  notes. They are an example of a handler that uses the database; replace them
  with your own.
{% endif %}
An absent, malformed, inactive, wrong-issuer, wrong-audience, or subject-less
token receives `401 Unauthorized`. A ZITADEL discovery/introspection failure
receives `503 Service Unavailable`; the API does not accept a token when it
cannot verify it.

Every error is JSON, `{"error":{"code":"forbidden","message":"..."}}`. Handlers
return `ApiError` (`src/error.rs`), which has a variant for each status you are
likely to need.

## Roles

Introspection tells the API the user's roles in this project, and
`AuthenticatedUser` carries them as `roles`; no scope beyond the project audience
that the web apps already request is needed. A handler requires one with
`require_role`, which answers `403` otherwise:

```rust
async fn publish(Extension(user): Extension<AuthenticatedUser>) -> Result<(), ApiError> {
    user.require_role("publisher")?;
    // ...
    Ok(())
}
```

Create the roles on the ZITADEL project (Console, **Roles**) and grant them to
users there; `has_role` is the non-failing check. Both claim forms ZITADEL uses
(`urn:zitadel:iam:org:project:roles` and the one scoped to this project's ID)
are read.

{% if database %}## Database

This API was generated with a PostgreSQL database. `moon run data:up` starts the
PostgreSQL of the data project (the `dev` and `test` tasks run it first) and
applies `db/init.sql`, which creates this API's own database and login role. The
connection string is `DATABASE_URL` in `.env`.

- `migrations/` is the schema. It is embedded in the binary and applied when the
  API starts. Add `migrations/0002_<what>.sql` for a change, and never edit one
  that has shipped.
- Queries use sqlx's runtime API (`sqlx::query_as`), so building needs neither a
  database nor `DATABASE_URL`.
- `src/notes.rs` is the example: handlers that read `AuthenticatedUser` and the
  pool from the router, and `#[sqlx::test]` tests. Each of those tests gets a
  throwaway database made from `DATABASE_URL`, with `migrations/` applied, and
  dropped afterwards, which is why the role has `CREATEDB`.

### In production

Give the container `DATABASE_URL`. The image needs nothing else: the migrations
are inside the binary.

The default build has no TLS support, so it connects in plain text. Note that
`sslmode=prefer`, the default, does so silently, and `sslmode=require` fails
with "built without TLS support". That is fine on a private network; over the
internet add TLS in `Cargo.toml`:

```toml
sqlx = { version = "0.8", default-features = false, features = ["runtime-tokio", "postgres", "migrate", "macros", "chrono", "tls-rustls-aws-lc-rs"] }
```

and ask for it in the URL. `?sslmode=require` encrypts the connection but does
not check who answers. `?sslmode=verify-full` checks the server's certificate
against the public CAs, or against your own with
`&sslrootcert=/run/secrets/db-ca.pem` (mount that file into the container). The
production image still builds, at about 17 MB.

{% endif %}## Local ZITADEL setup

From the repository root, run:

```bash
bun run setup
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:dev
```

`bun run setup` starts ZITADEL, creates this service's API application (Private
Key JWT) in the workspace's ZITADEL project, downloads its JSON key to the path
in `ZITADEL_API_KEY_FILE` (`secrets/` is ignored by Git), and sets
`ZITADEL_ISSUER` and `ZITADEL_PROJECT_ID` in this app's `.env`. Every API gets
its own application and key.

To set it up by hand instead, create an API application with JWT
authentication in the same ZITADEL project as the web apps, add a JSON key,
store the downloaded file at `ZITADEL_API_KEY_FILE`, and set
`ZITADEL_PROJECT_ID` in this app's `.env`. The web apps request the audience
scope `urn:zitadel:iam:org:project:id:<PROJECT_ID>:aud`, so their access tokens
carry this project.

The generated `.env.example` sets the listener port selected during generation.
The template fetches OIDC discovery metadata from the shared `ZITADEL_ISSUER`
at startup and uses the discovered introspection endpoint. Introspection uses
a short-lived RS256 client assertion (Private Key JWT) signed by the API
application's downloaded key. The user access token can be opaque or JWT; Axum
does not parse it locally.

## Development and tests

```bash
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:check
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:test
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:build
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:docker   # production image
```

The image holds only a static binary on distroless (about 15 MB) and listens
on `0.0.0.0:4000` as a non-root user. Give it `ZITADEL_ISSUER` and
`ZITADEL_PROJECT_ID`, and mount the key file at
`/run/secrets/zitadel-api-key.json`, readable by that user. The repository's
`deploy/` folder runs it behind HTTPS.

The auth middleware tests use a mock introspection service. They cover all 12
temporary test pairings (four web-app token sources by three API instances),
as well as inactive tokens, wrong audiences, and ZITADEL outages. No generated
test application is kept in the repository.
